# Performance & Cross-Device Engineering Rules

This project is a Vite + React 18 + TypeScript + Tailwind app. The user
base includes low-end Android devices common in Southeast Asian
markets, so performance on constrained hardware is a first-class
concern, not late-stage polish.

**The canonical, full-detail checklist lives in [`AGENTS.md`](mdc:AGENTS.md).
Read that file before making non-trivial changes.** This .mdc surfaces
the always-on rules that should shape every implementation.

## Always apply

- **Animate only `transform` and `opacity`.** Avoid `filter`,
  `box-shadow`, `border-radius`, `width`, `height`, `top`, `left`
  on animated elements — they trigger layout or paint.
- **Throttle scroll and pointermove handlers with `requestAnimationFrame`.**
  Coalesce per-event work to one tick per frame. Always
  `cancelAnimationFrame` on unmount.
- **Debounce input/search/resize handlers.** Use `setTimeout` for
  intentional delays (debounce, post-mount focus); `requestAnimationFrame`
  for visual updates.
- **Prefer `IntersectionObserver`** over scroll listeners for
  visibility detection. Prefer `ResizeObserver` over window resize for
  element size changes.
- **`backdrop-filter` is expensive.** Don't use it on large surfaces
  that repaint frequently (sticky headers, full-page overlays).
  Replace with a higher-opacity background. OK on small floating
  pills/strips.
- **Lazy-load all routes via `React.lazy()`** wrapped in `Suspense`.
  Initial bundle budget: under 170 kB gzipped for the matched route.
- **Set explicit `width` and `height` on `<img>` and `<video>`** to
  reserve space and prevent CLS. Use `aspect-ratio` for responsive
  containers.
- **Eager-load LCP candidates; lazy-load below-the-fold** with
  `loading="lazy"` and `decoding="async"`.
- **Use semantic HTML.** `<button>` instead of `<div onclick>`,
  `<header>/<main>/<nav>` instead of unlabeled `<div>`s.
- **Clean up.** Remove event listeners, clear intervals/timeouts,
  cancel rAFs, nullify large refs on unmount.
- **Use `transform: translateZ(0)` or `will-change` deliberately.**
  Each promoted layer costs GPU memory. Apply just before animation
  and remove after.
- **Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`** — skip or shorten animations
  for users who request it.
- **Test at mobile (375), tablet (768), desktop (1280) viewports.** A
  `useBreakpoint` hook exists at `src/hooks/useBreakpoint.ts`.

## Performance budgets

- TTI on mid-range mobile + 4G: **under 3.5s**
- LCP: **under 2.5s** | INP: **under 200ms** | CLS: **under 0.1**
- JavaScript per route: **under 170 kB gzipped**
- Animation: **consistent 60fps; never below 30fps** on target hardware

## When in doubt

Read [`AGENTS.md`](mdc:AGENTS.md) for the full checklist organized by
